italize on it i  look forward to the day where we don't  have to have uh the first first black  anything i look forward to that day  we're certainly not there yet that's why  we wanted to celebrate you on this  juneteenth do you did you feel or do you  feel stress or pressure on yourself  because you are a first in that position  um i i think at times um i the pressure  for me though is to perform i mean i  have a job to do and i need to do it  well but there's so many people that  have made this opportunity possible for  me i don't want to let them down people  like the tuskegee airmen like you  mentioned nate  i remember this  one event  i was at it was a tuskegee airman  convention and i had showed up in our  chief of staff mark welsh at the time  general mark welsh  he met me there he told me that i was  going to be promoted to three star but  to keep it quiet  and i spent that  that cocktail our time just talking to  these original tuskegee airmen didn't  tell him i was going to be promoted  right but i was just on cloud nine  but then when general welsh he was the  keynote speaker it was when i was a  commander of eighth air force which is  all of our bomber forces yeah um he gave  this great speech about the tuskegee  airmen and then he goes where's rich  clark are you sitting in the audience  where are you rich and he asked me to  come up  and i had no idea what he was going to  do and he brought me up and he's and he  looked out to these original tuskegee  airmen he goes  would you have ever imagined when you  were flying missions in world war ii  that one day  the commander of the bombers that you're  escorting would be a black man yeah and  and those tuskegee airmen it it was so  humbling they started to stand up and  clap and clap and i realized it wasn't  about me  it was about one person who was a part  of their legacy and i it put some uh  honestly some pressure on me to to pay  it forward but we're clapping for you  today and clearly you take the job